// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders a form with several errors 1`] = `
"Form rejected — 2 errors. Nothing was rendered; fix the errors and call again.

ERROR   unknown_field_type · sections[0].fields[0].type "env"
  Unknown field type "single_selct". Field types are a closed vocabulary (§4.1) — the
  renderer can only branch on what it understands. Use one of "single_select",
  "multi_select", "boolean", "rank", "number", "slider", "allocation", "date", "date_range",
  "matrix", "repeatable", "table", "short_text", "long_text", "info", "computed". Did you
  mean "single_select"?

ERROR   unknown_property · sections[1].fields[0] "cutover"
  Unknown property "helpText". The meta-schema is closed: remove it, or move what it said
  into free text — "label", "description" or a per-path note (§4.1/§4.4)."
`;

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders a matrix that cannot be drawn 1`] = `
"Form rejected — 3 errors. Nothing was rendered; fix the errors and call again.

ERROR   matrix_cell_options_required · sections[1].fields[2].cellOptions "fls"
  Matrix "fls" has cellType "single_select" but declares no "cellOptions". A select cell
  needs the option set once for the whole grid — that is what makes cycle/paint possible
  (§5.5). Add "cellOptions": [{ "value": "R", "label": "Read" }, …].

ERROR   matrix_constraint_unknown_member · sections[1].fields[2].constraints[0] "fls"
  Constraint 0 of matrix "fls" names row "Margin__c", which the matrix does not declare.
  Matrix row and column ids are agent-declared and fixed for the life of the view (§4.5);
  declared rows are "Discount__c".

ERROR   matrix_constraint_empty · sections[1].fields[2].constraints[0] "fls"
  Constraint 0 of matrix "fls" says nothing: it narrows no values and does not freeze the
  cell. Add "allowed": [...] to restrict the cell, or "readOnly": true to freeze it — and a
  free-text "reason", which is what the renderer shows when a bulk apply skips the cell
  (§5.5)."
`;

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders a rules-only failure, naming every index 1`] = `
"Form rejected — 3 errors, 3 warnings. Nothing was rendered; fix the errors and call again.

ERROR   rule_payload_required · rules[0].then
  Rule 0 uses "filter_options" without saying which options survive. Add "options": [...]
  listing the option values that remain — that is the half of the mechanic people forget,
  and without it the rule does nothing (§4.6).

ERROR   rule_in_requires_array · rules[1].when.value
  Rule 1 uses op "in", so "value" must be an array of the option values that satisfy it, as
  in "value": ["aws", "gcp"].

ERROR   rule_default_not_an_option · rules[1].then.value
  Rule 1 defaults "scopeChoice" to "half_of_it", which is not one of its declared values
  ("accounts", "everything"). set_default writes only into empty fields (§4.6), and it can
  only write a value the field can hold.

WARNING rule_payload_ignored · rules[2].then.options
  Rule 2 carries "options" but its action is "hide", which ignores them. Only
  "filter_options" narrows an option set.

WARNING rule_cycle · rules[1]
  Rules 1 → 2 → 1 could cycle: each one changes a field the next one reads. Evaluation
  re-runs the flat list until the view is stable and stops at an iteration cap (§4.6), so a
  cycle means the rendered state may depend on where the cap lands. Break the loop — usually
  one of these rules should read a different field, or be folded into the other.

WARNING rule_cycle · rules[2]
  Rules 2 → 3 → 2 could cycle: each one changes a field the next one reads. Evaluation
  re-runs the flat list until the view is stable and stops at an iteration cap (§4.6), so a
  cycle means the rendered state may depend on where the cap lands. Break the loop — usually
  one of these rules should read a different field, or be folded into the other."
`;

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders an answer map that came back malformed 1`] = `
"Form rejected — 2 errors. Nothing was rendered; fix the errors and call again.

ERROR   malformed · answers["env"].value
  An answer with state "empty" must not carry a "value" — "not rendered = empty" means an
  empty answer holds nothing (§4.6). Drop the value, or set the state to "answered".

ERROR   unknown_answer_state · answers["notes"].state
  Unknown answer state "pending". There are exactly two (§4.3): "answered", "empty" — defer
  is an ordinary agent-defined option value, not a third state."
`;

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders the multi_select membership errors, each with the op to use instead 1`] = `
"Form rejected — 5 errors. Nothing was rendered; fix the errors and call again.

ERROR   rule_in_on_a_set · rules[0].when.op
  Rule 0 uses "in" against field "addons" of type "multi_select", which holds a SET of
  values. "in" compares the field's whole value to each candidate, so against a multi_select
  it can never be true and the rule would never fire. For one chosen option use "contains"
  ("value": "gdpr_pack"); for a list, "contains_any" / "contains_all" / "contains_none"
  ("value": ["gdpr_pack","audit_log"]); for the whole selection exactly, "eq" with the full
  array.

ERROR   rule_membership_needs_a_set · rules[1].when.op
  Rule 1 uses "contains" against field "region" of type "single_select", but that field
  holds ONE value, not a set. Use "eq"/"neq" for one value, or "in" for several — "contains"
  is for multi_select.

ERROR   rule_membership_takes_one_value · rules[2].when.value
  Rule 2 uses op "contains", which tests ONE option value against what the field holds, so
  "value" must be that value and not a list — as in "value": "pepperoni". For a list, say
  which you mean: "contains_all" or "contains_any".

ERROR   rule_set_op_takes_a_list · rules[3].when.value
  Rule 3 uses op "contains_any", which compares a LIST of option values against what the
  field holds, so "value" must be an array — as in "value": ["gdpr_pack", "audit_log"]. For
  a single value, use "contains" or "not_contains".

ERROR   rule_set_op_empty_list · rules[4].when.value
  Rule 4 uses op "contains_all" with an empty list, which asks nothing: "contains_all" and
  "contains_none" would hold for every answer and "contains_any" for none. List the option
  values the branch depends on, or use "empty"/"filled" if what you meant was whether the
  field was answered at all."
`;

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders the ordinal teaching error — the §4.5 rule that prevents corruption 1`] = `
"Form rejected — 1 error. Nothing was rendered; fix the errors and call again.

ERROR   ordinal_path · prefill["stakeholders[2].owner"]
  Path "stakeholders[2].owner" addresses row 2 of the repeatable "stakeholders" by position.
  Rows of a repeatable are user-mutable, so positions shift: deleting a row would silently
  re-point every path anchored at [2], including notes. Use the minted row id instead —
  "stakeholders[r_7f3a].owner". Ordinals are for display, never for persistence (§4.5)."
`;

exports[`formatDiagnostics > renders warnings on a form that still renders 1`] = `
"Form accepted — 2 warnings. It renders as-is; the warnings are smells worth a second look.

WARNING no_prefill · (form root)
  This form prefills nothing. Reviewing is cheap and authoring is expensive: the agent
  should infer what it can and render a proposal with provenance on every value. "If there's
  nothing to prefill, the form probably shouldn't exist" (§4.7) — consider asking in chat
  instead.

WARNING section_single_field · sections[1] "timing"
  Section "timing" holds one field. "Never a section with one field" (§4.8) — a section is a
  unit of the left rail and of section status, and one field does not earn a heading. Merge
  it into a neighbouring section, or add the fields that belong with it."
`;
